WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.136 --> 00:00:03.146
As you are modernizing your enterprise, how do you mitigate the risks

00:00:03.146 --> 00:00:07.076
and vulnerabilities associated with transitioning into a container architecture?

00:00:07.666 --> 00:00:11.066
Hi, my name is Charles and I'm a Cloud Developer here with IBM Cloud.

00:00:11.526 --> 00:00:15.386
Today, I'm going to give you a general overview
and an introduction to how you can begin

00:00:15.386 --> 00:00:16.986
to secure your container environment.

00:00:17.806 --> 00:00:23.406
Let's say that you're an enterprise and
you're using VMs to host your application code

00:00:23.816 --> 00:00:25.986
and you want to transition
into using containers.

00:00:27.086 --> 00:00:29.856
Containers are really great
because they allow you

00:00:30.206 --> 00:00:34.796
to smoothen the application development pipeline
and they can be deployed just about anywhere.

00:00:35.456 --> 00:00:39.116
However, because of this flexibility of
being able to deploy just about anywhere,

00:00:39.516 --> 00:00:44.656
a whole new variety of attack services
exist surrounding the container environment.

00:00:45.156 --> 00:00:53.506
Some of these include the images that you use
to build your containers; the image registries

00:00:54.576 --> 00:01:02.366
that we use to store our images
in; the container run times

00:01:03.086 --> 00:01:06.686
which we use to execute our containers.

00:01:09.796 --> 00:01:15.016
And, the orchestration platforms which we use

00:01:15.016 --> 00:01:18.416
to manage the lifecycle of
multiple containers with.

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:30.246
And lastly, the host OS which manages the Docker
client and the Docker daemon and the kernel

00:01:30.246 --> 00:01:33.166
of which is shared with our
running Docker containers.

00:01:34.336 --> 00:01:39.626
So, when we look at images as a potential
vulnerability, one of the best things

00:01:39.626 --> 00:01:42.216
that we can do to address this
vulnerability is to make sure

00:01:42.216 --> 00:01:44.436
that we're keeping our images up to date.

00:01:45.306 --> 00:01:48.666
And the reason this is so
important is because images that are

00:01:48.666 --> 00:01:52.606
up to date typically have the latest
security patches which protect you

00:01:52.606 --> 00:01:54.366
from the latest vulnerabilities that they track.

00:01:55.006 --> 00:02:00.276
We also want to make sure that we're regularly
scanning our images so that we can keep track

00:02:00.276 --> 00:02:03.106
of any new vulnerabilities that
may not have already been caught.

00:02:03.726 --> 00:02:07.006
And we also want to make sure
that we're signing our images.

00:02:07.436 --> 00:02:12.236
And signing an image will essentially create
a digital fingerprint that you can use

00:02:12.236 --> 00:02:16.156
to cryptographically test
and verify whoever it was

00:02:16.156 --> 00:02:18.336
that was trying to manipulate the image with.

00:02:18.866 --> 00:02:22.746
Now, image registries are a potential
vulnerability, because they contain all

00:02:22.746 --> 00:02:24.946
of these images which contain
your application code.

00:02:25.406 --> 00:02:28.316
And so in order to keep them
secure, you want to make sure

00:02:28.316 --> 00:02:34.456
that you're keeping your image registries
private and a private registry really is one

00:02:34.456 --> 00:02:39.156
that gives you absolute control over
the types of images, how many images

00:02:39.466 --> 00:02:43.126
and the access control of the users
that are accessing the registry.

00:02:43.626 --> 00:02:48.076
You also want to make sure that you're
regularly monitoring your registry

00:02:48.386 --> 00:02:52.466
so that you can track any vulnerabilities
that may come about, and you want to make sure

00:02:52.466 --> 00:02:59.336
that the host server for the registry
is secure so that you can protect it

00:02:59.336 --> 00:03:01.406
from being compromised and
you can protect your images

00:03:01.406 --> 00:03:03.596
and the application code from being accessed.

00:03:04.026 --> 00:03:08.516
When it comes to the container run time,
run times can be a little bit tricky

00:03:08.516 --> 00:03:16.536
because historically security tools related
to containers monitor more the communication

00:03:16.536 --> 00:03:18.376
around them rather than what's happening inside.

00:03:18.686 --> 00:03:24.246
So, one of the best things you can do is to
make sure that you're app security is up to par

00:03:24.926 --> 00:03:29.646
and you also want to make sure that you
are monitoring your network protocols

00:03:29.646 --> 00:03:30.826
and your network payloads.

00:03:31.406 --> 00:03:34.836
You also want to make sure that
you're monitoring the host as well.

00:03:35.786 --> 00:03:38.666
When it comes to orchestration
softwares, these are platforms

00:03:38.666 --> 00:03:41.546
such as Kubernetes or container
platforms like OCP.

00:03:41.546 --> 00:03:47.376
And what's great about these is that they
include lots of access control capabilities

00:03:47.376 --> 00:03:53.626
which means that you can easily set limits
not just on the number of privileged users

00:03:53.626 --> 00:03:55.726
that you have -- which is
an important thing to do --

00:03:56.046 --> 00:04:00.376
but also the amount of privilege that you give
any one user so that if the user is compromised,

00:04:00.836 --> 00:04:04.316
they're not able to attack your
entire orchestration platform.

00:04:04.316 --> 00:04:12.836
You also want to make sure that you are
monitoring your orchestration platform as well.

00:04:13.016 --> 00:04:18.766
And that you're monitoring the pod
communication within the orchestration platform.

00:04:19.576 --> 00:04:24.696
When it comes to the host OS, this is
potentially one of the greatest vulnerabilities

00:04:24.696 --> 00:04:29.106
that you can have in your container
environment because the host OS

00:04:29.316 --> 00:04:32.416
if compromised will give the
attacker essentially access

00:04:32.416 --> 00:04:33.856
to your entire application environment.

00:04:34.186 --> 00:04:38.906
So, in order to help to mitigate
this, it's good to have a slim OS,

00:04:38.906 --> 00:04:41.866
something like SE Linux is a great option.

00:04:41.866 --> 00:04:49.886
You also want to make sure that you have access
control implemented within your OS and you want

00:04:49.886 --> 00:04:53.336
to make sure that you're also
regularly monitoring the OS as well

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:58.116
for any vulnerabilities to
protect it from being compromised.

00:04:58.726 --> 00:05:02.006
And so these are the a few of the
potential vulnerabilities that exist

00:05:02.006 --> 00:05:04.006
in the space surrounding your container.

00:05:04.456 --> 00:05:08.066
When it comes to protecting the container
itself, there's a lot of great open source tools

00:05:08.066 --> 00:05:13.106
that exist and one of them falls
under the category of scanning.

00:05:13.106 --> 00:05:18.296
It's sort of the de facto option
when it comes to scanning tools

00:05:18.296 --> 00:05:20.016
for containers and it's called Docker Bench.

00:05:20.656 --> 00:05:26.486
And Docker Bench is great because it's
really just a simple script that runs

00:05:26.486 --> 00:05:28.756
and it will test your container against dozens

00:05:28.756 --> 00:05:32.216
of different container production
deployment best practices,

00:05:32.216 --> 00:05:35.086
and it will let you know how
your container does against it.

00:05:35.916 --> 00:05:43.356
Open Scap is another great option which it sort
of will collect and analyze different...oh,

00:05:43.356 --> 00:05:49.056
sorry, it will allow you to create schedules
around continuous scanning for your container.

00:05:50.966 --> 00:05:57.096
Now, when it comes to monitoring tools, a
really great option is called Prometheus,

00:05:57.516 --> 00:06:02.226
and Prometheus is great because
this one will allow you to collect

00:06:02.226 --> 00:06:06.406
and analyze different metrics around
communication between different node end points.

00:06:06.976 --> 00:06:17.066
Lastly, when it comes to firewall tools, Cilium
is a fantastic option because it will allow you

00:06:17.066 --> 00:06:21.306
to analyze communication in your
network and also the communication

00:06:21.306 --> 00:06:23.346
between all the different
application services that you have.

00:06:24.106 --> 00:06:25.176
So, just to recap.

00:06:25.176 --> 00:06:29.656
We've gone over a few of the
vulnerabilities that exist in the environment

00:06:29.656 --> 00:06:34.346
around the container and then we've gone
over some really great open source tools

00:06:34.346 --> 00:06:35.626
that can help to secure your container.

00:06:35.876 --> 00:06:36.436
Thank you.

00:06:36.696 --> 00:06:38.896
If you have questions, please
drop us a line below.

00:06:39.306 --> 00:06:42.776
If you want to see more videos like this
